Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 06:54:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] video, builtins: SubtitleShiftUp(step) and
 SubtitleShiftDown(step) move the subtitles one fixed step

A press of a key bound to subtitleshiftup or subtitleshiftdown moves the subtitles by the distance of
a moving speed (CPlayerController, UTILS::MOVING_SPEED, 100 to 300 a second): one pixel for a single
press, faster only while the key repeats. A remote that sends single presses (a TV remote over CEC,
a phone app, a menu entry of a skin) then needs dozens of presses to lift a line of text above the
bar of a 2.39:1 picture on a 16:9 screen, which is what moving them is mostly for.

The built-ins now take "step" next to "save", in any order and case, and give the action every
option they were given (before, only a first "save"): SubtitleShiftUp(step) moves the subtitles a
thirty-second of the height of the interface (33 pixels at 1080 lines, 67 at 2160), up to the same
limits as before; SubtitleShiftUp(step,save) also keeps the position for every video. A key map can
bind them: <up>SubtitleShiftUp(step)</up>. Without "step" nothing changes. The options are read by
one small function of its own (video/SubtitleShiftStep.h) that a host test covers.

The idea of a fixed step is CorentinB's (SubtitleShiftUp(step) in another build); written anew here.
